      "id": 1,
      "text": "Greenwood began as Smocktown, honoring the Smock brothers, and was renamed Greenfield in 1825 before settling on its current name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwood%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Greenwood was incorporated as a town under Indiana law in 1864, marking its formal municipal status.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwood%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Electric Indianapolis Interurban Railway System made Greenwood a key cog, with the line graded by Smith along what is now Madison Avenue.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwood%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Polk Canning Company drove Greenwood’s early growth; it was later acquired by Stokely Company, which operated it until 1959.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://indyencyclopedia.org/greenwood/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Within Greenwood city limits, the Greenwood Public Library serves residents, and a separate branch of the Johnson County Public Library also operates.",
